extraction_notes: "WB20 Canada printed 85-86 (cache p086-p087, cache page = printed folio + 1), read off the text layer; both pages were rendered and read beside it. Heading Tundra Ranger Scout O.C.C. on printed 85, stat block headed The Tundra Ranger Scout O.C.C. (also known as the Snowman and Forest Shadow); the bonuses run over onto printed 86. || GROUP: the book has no Men at Arms heading; the chapter is The Tundra Rangers (printed 78-90) and the entry calls the Scout more than just a soldier in a para-military force, so occ_group is men-of-arms and men_of_arms is true. The entry prints no S.D.C. base; +4D6 S.D.C. is listed under O.C.C. bonuses and is stored as a pool bonus. || XP: the Worldly Sasquatch and Tundra Ranger Scout ladder on printed 192, as supplied from a render. || BONUSES: +1D4 P.E. and +2 P.S., +1 initiative, +2 roll, +3 pull punch stored; +4 vs cold is stored as saves.other; +1 vs Horror Factor at levels 2, 4, 7, 10 and 13 as five at_level entries. || LANGUAGES: Speaks Inuit, plus one Native American tongue and one of choice, all +15%. The catalog has no Language: Inuit row and no Native American language row, so the line is stored as three Language: Other picks at +15%. || SKILL READINGS: Basic Math is Mathematics: Basic; Speaks American/English at 98% is Language: Native Tongue; Literate in American at 84% +1% per level is Literacy: Native Language at 84 with per_level 1; Pilot Hovercycle, Jet Pack or Hovervehicle is a pick among Hovercycles, Skycycles & Rocket Bikes, Jet Packs and Hover Craft (ground); Tracking (humanoids) is Tracking (people); Track Animals is Track & Trap Animals; Lore: Indians is Lore: American Indians; Swim and Climb are Swimming and Climbing. Camouflage +15% in all environments is stored as the flat +15%. Hand to Hand: Expert, no others available. || RELATED: Electrical Basic only is Basic Electronics; Rogue none other than Cardsharp and Prowl (+5%), where the catalog files Prowl under Physical; Pilot except robots, ships and aircraft excludes the robot and power armor rows, Boat: Ships, the submersible and warship rows, and every aircraft and spacecraft row; Jet Packs, hovercycles and Military: Tanks & APCs stay available (a reading). This book files riding under Pilot, so the catalog Horsemanship category is offered as General and Exotic Animals only. || EQUIPMENT: Standard Equipment, Vehicle and Money all say Standard and point at Notes on the Tundra Rangers, printed 82-83, so that list is stored: Legion Tundra armor and the thermal-armor suit (catalog rows from this book), six E-Clips, a Vibro-Blade as a pick of three since the book names no kind, two field uniforms, a dress uniform, cleated insulated combat boots, casual and dress clothing, thermal gloves on the plain gloves row, the high-impact helmet with built-in communicator on the communications-helmet row, tinted goggles, the 100 feet of nylon cord on the cord row, utility belt, scarf, air filter, backpack, bedroll, duffle bag or saddlebag as a pick, flashlight, distancing binoculars, language translator, note pad, 1D4 markers, 1D4 signal flares, two canteens, 6 weeks of rations as six of the week-sized food-rations row, first aid kit, and a horse or hovercycle as a pick (printed 83: a horse or hovercycle are pretty standard). NOT stored, no catalog row: the energy rifle itself and one weapon with two E-Clips for each W.P. (the book names no models; printed 187 points at the core book early Coalition weapons), dress boots, pick-hatchet, tundra survival kit, 20 climbing anchors, small M.D.C. ceramic mallet, web vest, winter cap. The multi-optics goggle is a special request, not issue, and is left out. || MONEY: Standard is a salary of 1500 credits per month plus room, board and gear (printed 83); the book prints no starting savings, so one month of salary is stored as starting_money, a reading. || SCOUT ITEMS (printed 86), stored: survival knife, skinning knives (no number printed; two stored), pocket knife, hand axe, lighter, flint for the flint and tinder box, 1D4 small sacks, four medium sacks, one large sack, two more canteens or water skins and 1D4 more markers (folded into the standard rows as 4 and 2D4), 10 feet of thin nylon cord. NOT stored: cooking utensils and 20 feet of wire have no catalog row; the 50 feet of additional 600 lb cord shares the cord row with the standard 100 feet; a handful of personal items. || The Code of the Tundra Rangers (printed 81) is paraphrased in the body."
---

## Lore

The Scout is the Tundra Rangers'' ranger in the older sense of the word: the
one who knows the cold country itself. Forest, tundra and Arctic ice are
home ground, and a Scout knows how to travel them unseen, live off them, and
turn weather and terrain against an enemy. They also know the native
peoples, D-Bees and monsters who share that country.

Where a Wilderness Scout uses such knowledge to get by, a Ranger Scout uses
it as a soldier does: laying and spotting ambushes and traps, camouflage,
reconnaissance behind enemy lines and gathering intelligence. Search and
rescue in the mountains and on the Arctic flats is as much the job as
fighting, and a Scout is expected to coordinate others and keep people alive
in a land full of supernatural horrors and human opportunists.

Nearly half of the Scouts are Native Americans, Inuit among them. The
Rangers call them Snowmen and Forest Shadows.

## The Code of the Tundra Rangers

- Keep the people of Canada safe, and keep the peace.
- See tyranny for what it is, whatever shape it takes, and stand against it.
- Help and protect those who need it, but leave them able to manage for
  themselves; that is the best help there is.
- Never let up in the pursuit of justice. The guilty answer for what they did.
- Welcome other beliefs and ways of living, and value what all peaceful,
  thinking beings share.
- Do not doubt the good being done. Believe in yourself and others will too.
- Make no threat or demand that you cannot and will not back with force.
- Keep your word and never betray a trust; the trust people give the Rangers
  is worth more than any reward.
- Never compromise a fellow Ranger, and never speak for another.
- One person can change things.
- No Tundra Ranger stands alone.

## GM Notes

Standard issue the sheet does not list, because the catalog has no row for it:
the energy rifle itself, a weapon for every W.P. with two E-Clips apiece, dress
boots, a pick-hatchet, a tundra survival kit, 20 climbing anchors, a small
M.D.C. ceramic mallet, a web vest and a winter cap. Many Rangers also wear a
multi-optics goggle, by special request.

Heavier weapons, armor, power armor, explosives, sensors, generators and
extra supplies may be issued for a particular assignment, at the commanding
officer''s discretion. Veterans collect personal non-standard gear as well.

Pay is 1500 credits a month on top of room, board, medical care and
equipment, and rises with rank and hazard duty (the book points at Coalition
War Campaign pages 50-54 for rank and pay). The sheet starts the character
with one month of salary.

A Ranger checks in at a station at least every two months and returns to
Regina or the Fort Smith headquarters at least once a year. Breaking the Code
or disobeying orders means a dishonorable discharge.

Scout items the sheet does not list: cooking utensils, 50 feet of additional
600 lb test cord (the standard 100 feet is on the cord row), wire (20 feet)
and a few personal items.

Languages: the Scout speaks Inuit, one other Native American tongue and one
language of choice, each at +15%. The sheet stores these as three free
language picks.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'tundra-ranger-scout');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'tundra-ranger-scout';

